---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The accountable layer - Sotiris Iliadis"
  description="Why I built the Article 50 UI Pattern Kit. The EU AI Act's transparency rules are a design problem, not just a legal one - and the human-accountable layer is interface work."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[680px] px-6 pt-28 pb-24">
    <a
      href="/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[10px] font-geist font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-[#6B6560] hover:text-[#1A1410] transition-colors focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-[#1A1410]"
    >
      ← Sotiris Iliadis
    </a>

    <p class="mt-12 text-[10px] font-geist font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-[#6B6560]">
      Essay · 2026
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif font-bold leading-[0.95] tracking-tight text-[#1A1410]" style="font-size: clamp(40px, 7vw, 68px)">
      The <span class="italic text-[#C85A0C]">accountable</span> layer
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-geist font-light text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#6B6560]">
      Why I built the Article 50 UI Pattern Kit - and why the EU AI Act's transparency rules are a
      design problem, not just a legal one.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-[11px] font-geist font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#6B6560]">
      By Sotiris Iliadis · 4 min read
    </p>

    <hr class="mt-8 border-t border-[#1A1410]/10" />

    <div class="prose-body mt-10 font-geist text-[#1A1410]">
      <p>
        On 2 August 2026, the transparency rules of the EU AI Act - Article 50 - become enforceable.
        Most teams I talk to are treating that as a legal problem: a policy to write, a box to tick, a
        risk for someone else to own. I think that reading is wrong, and I think it is wrong in a way
        that matters for anyone who designs software.
      </p>
      <p>
        Article 50 does not live in a policy document. It lives in the interface. It is the moment a
        person needs to know they are talking to an AI and not a human. The moment a piece of content
        has to be marked as generated. The moment a system that reads emotion has to say so, before it
        acts. The moment an automated decision still has to pass through a person who can say no. Those
        are not clauses. They are buttons, labels, notices, and review steps - and someone has to design
        and build them.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I did. The Article 50 UI Pattern Kit is four accessible React components for the parts of the
        Act that live in the UI. Open-source, MIT, WCAG 2.1 AA. This is the thinking behind each one,
        because the decisions are the interesting part.
      </p>

      <h2>AI disclosure that does not disappear</h2>
      <p>
        The easy version of "tell people it is AI" is a toast that shows once and slides away. That
        fails the moment the user scrolls, refreshes, or arrives mid-conversation. So the disclosure
        badge is persistent, and the full sentence is always available to a screen reader even when the
        visible label is shortened to a chip. Meaning never depends on the icon or the colour alone.
        Disclosure that a blind user cannot reach is not disclosure.
      </p>

      <h2>A label people and machines can read</h2>
      <p>
        Article 50(4) has no transition period for new content, which makes labelling generated media
        the most time-sensitive pattern in the kit. But a label a human can see is only half the job.
        The content label is also machine-readable - it carries a data attribute other software can
        detect - so provenance survives the screenshot, the repost, and the scrape. Visible for people,
        structured for machines.
      </p>

      <h2>An emotion notice that keeps its promise</h2>
      <p>
        If a system estimates how you feel, you are owed a notice before it acts on you - and the duty
        to inform does not end the second you dismiss it. So the emotion-recognition notice is a
        labelled region, never a modal that traps your focus, and when you acknowledge it, it collapses
        into a small persistent indicator rather than vanishing. You are told, and you stay told.
      </p>

      <h2>A gate where a human is the final authority</h2>
      <p>
        This is the ethical centre of the kit. AI proposes; a named human decides. The review gate holds
        an AI-suggested action until a person approves, edits, or rejects it. Confidence is shown as a
        number and a bar - never colour alone - and a low-confidence result is flagged with an icon and
        the words, so the handoff to human judgement is legible instead of buried under a fluent
        sentence.
      </p>

      <h2>Watching the four work as one</h2>
      <p>
        Patterns in isolation are easy to nod at. So I built a live demo where they run together as a
        single feature: a tone reader you can actually type into. It streams its reasoning before it
        commits, shows how sure it is, sends the uncertain calls to the gate, and - the part I am most
        attached to - refuses to answer when the input is nonsense, instead of fabricating a confident
        guess.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Live embed, broken slightly wider than the reading measure -->
    <figure class="mt-10 -mx-6 md:-mx-16">
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden ring-1 ring-[#1A1410]/[0.10] bg-[#F5F2ED] shadow-[0_20px_60px_-24px_rgba(26,20,16,0.35)]">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-[#1A1410]/[0.08] bg-[#EDE9E3]">
          <span class="flex gap-1.5" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-[#1A1410]/15"></span>
            <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-[#1A1410]/15"></span>
            <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-[#F26C0D]/50"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="ml-2 text-[9px] md:text-[10px] font-geist font-bold tracking-[0.22em] uppercase text-[#6B6560] truncate">
            Live · article50-kit.pages.dev/demo
          </span>
        </div>
        <iframe
          src="https://article50-kit.pages.dev/embed"
          title="Live demo: Article 50 Tone Read - an accountable AI tone reader you can try"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full h-[560px] md:h-[640px] bg-[#F5F2ED] block"
        ></iframe>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 px-6 md:px-0 text-sm font-geist text-[#6B6560]">
        The live demo. Type a message, or try a sample - nothing you type leaves the page.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-body mt-12 font-geist text-[#1A1410]">
      <p>
        The model behind it is a small, deterministic classifier, not a live LLM, so every state
        including refusal can be shown on demand, at zero cost, with nothing leaving your browser.
        Swapping in a real model changes one function; the interface does not move.
      </p>

      <h2>Accessibility is not the decoration</h2>
      <p>
        Here is the part I will not soften: "accessible AI" that fails a contrast check is just a
        slogan. So I ran a real accessibility audit against the kit, it found genuine bugs, and I fixed
        every one - including a contrast failure the automated tool missed on a gradient background,
        which I only caught by doing the maths by hand. Zero violations, verified. If the pitch is that
        AI should be accountable and inclusive, the artifact has to survive the same test it asks
        everyone else to pass.
      </p>

      <h2>Where this leaves us</h2>
      <p>
        I keep landing on the same idea. AI does not remove the need for a human-accountable layer. It
        makes that layer the product - the surface where trust is either earned or quietly skipped. And
        that layer is interface work: disclosure, labelling, oversight, the honest refusal. It is
        exactly where a designer who codes should be standing.
      </p>
      <p>The kit is free. Take the patterns, ship them, and tell me what breaks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a
        href="https://article50-kit.pages.dev"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2.5 px-7 py-[13px] bg-[#F26C0D] text-[#1A1410] text-[10px] font-geist font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.3em] hover:bg-[#1A1410] hover:text-white transition-all duration-300 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-[#F26C0D]"
      >
        Open the Kit
        <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a
        href="https://article50-kit.pages.dev/demo"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2.5 px-7 py-[13px] border border-[#1A1410]/25 text-[#1A1410] text-[10px] font-geist font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.3em] hover:border-[#1A1410] hover:bg-[#1A1410]/5 transition-all duration-300 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-[#1A1410]"
      >
        Try the live demo
        <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .prose-body p {
      font-size: 1.125rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
      font-weight: 300;
      margin-top: 1.35rem;
    }
    .prose-body h2 {
      font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.4vw, 2rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      color: #1a1410;
      margin-top: 2.8rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }
    .prose-body p:first-child {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
